---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const description =
  'What The Open Accounts Receivable Collective Foundation collects, why, who else processes it, and how to get it corrected or removed.';
---

<Base title="Privacy" description={description}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <p class="overline">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy Notice</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This notice explains what the Foundation collects, why it collects it, who else handles it,
        and what you can ask us to do about it. It covers openarcollective.org,
        join.openarcollective.org, the Foundation's Discord server, and the email we send.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="short">
      <h2 id="short">The short version</h2>
      <p>
        The public website does not track you. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics, and loads
        nothing from a third party. You can read everything the Foundation publishes without telling
        us who you are, and nothing we publish requires membership to access. We do not track our
        email either.
      </p>
      <p>
        We collect personal information only when you give it to us, which happens when you apply
        for membership, when your organization signs the Statement of Support, or when you connect a
        Discord account. We do not sell it, we do not publish it, we do not use it for advertising, and
        we do not use it for lobbying or political advocacy.
      </p>

      <h2 id="visiting">When you visit openarcollective.org</h2>
      <p>
        The public website is a set of static files. It sets no cookies, uses no analytics service,
        and requests no scripts, fonts, or images from any other company. Fonts are served from our
        own domain. If a page links to GitHub, LinkedIn, or Discord, nothing is sent to those
        companies unless you click the link.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is served through Cloudflare, which handles the connection and, as part of
        delivering the page and protecting the site from attack, processes your IP address and
        request details. We do not receive a per-visitor report from that process, and we do not
        build profiles of visitors.
      </p>

      <h2 id="membership">When you apply for membership</h2>
      <p>
        The membership application at join.openarcollective.org asks for your first and last name,
        your email address, your job title, the organization you are affiliated with, and a link to
        your LinkedIn profile. It also records your confirmation that the information is truthful,
        your affirmation of the Foundation's mission, and your agreement to the Community
        Participation Terms, together with the version of those terms in force when you signed and
        the date you signed.
      </p>
      <p>
        We use that information to confirm you work in the accounts receivable industry, which is
        the one eligibility requirement for membership, and to administer your membership afterward.
        A director reviews each application, which means a person reads what you submitted. If we
        admit you, we issue a member number and keep the record of your membership. If we decline,
        we keep the record of the decision and its reason, because the Community Programs and
        Standards Policy gives you an appeal and an appeal needs a record.
      </p>
      <p>
        Members are not published. There is no public member roster, and we do not list your name,
        your employer, or your membership anywhere on the site.
      </p>

      <h2 id="supporters">When your organization signs the Statement of Support</h2>
      <p>
        The Statement of Support asks for your organization's legal name, the trade name it uses if
        that differs, its website, where it is registered, and the name, title, and email address of
        the person signing on its behalf. It records that person's confirmation that they have
        authority to sign, the organization's affirmation of the mission, agreement to the terms,
        the version signed, and the date.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your organization's name is published on the public Mission Supporter roster. That is the
        point of the program, and the form says so before you sign. The signer's name, title, and
        email address are not published. They are kept so we know who committed the organization and
        so we have somebody to contact about the listing.
      </p>

      <h2 id="discord">When you connect a Discord account</h2>
      <p>
        Admitted members can connect a Discord account to reach the members-only areas of the
        Foundation's server. Doing so asks Discord for permission to read your account identifier
        and the email address on your Discord account, and to add you to the Foundation's server. We
        store your Discord account identifier so we can match your Discord account to your
        membership. We do not read your messages, your other servers, or your friends list, and the
        permissions we request do not allow it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Discord is a separate company with its own privacy policy, and anything you post on the
        Foundation's server is handled under Discord's terms as well as ours. Connecting Discord is
        optional, and no part of your membership depends on it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="email">Email we send you</h2>
      <p>
        Some of our email is transactional, which means it is the mechanism rather than a message we
        chose to send. Confirming your address, telling you a decision, and issuing your member
        number are all transactional, and you cannot opt out of them while your application or
        membership is live.
      </p>
      <p>
        Admitted members are also added to the members-only email list. That is a separate thing, it
        is stated before you apply, and you can leave it at any time without affecting your
        membership. Every message on that list carries a link to stop receiving Foundation email.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not track our email. Open tracking and click tracking are both switched off, so we do
        not record whether you opened a message, when you opened it, or which links you followed,
        and the links in our email are our own rather than redirects through a tracking service. Our
        provider still reports whether a message was accepted for delivery or bounced, because that
        is how we learn an address is wrong, and that is the whole of what we see.
      </p>

      <h2 id="processors">Who else processes your information</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation is small and runs on other companies' infrastructure. The companies below
        process personal information on our behalf, each under its own agreement and privacy policy.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Cloudflare, for DNS, content delivery, and protection of both sites.</li>
        <li>
          Akamai, through its Linode service, which hosts the server behind
          join.openarcollective.org.
        </li>
        <li>Postmark, which sends the Foundation's email.</li>
        <li>Discord, which operates the community server.</li>
        <li>
          Google, whose Workspace service carries the Foundation's own mailboxes, including the
          address that receives applications for review.
        </li>
        <li>
          GitHub, which hosts the Foundation's source code and the published Mission Supporter
          roster.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We add a processor only when we need one, and we do not give any of them permission to use
        your information for their own purposes.
      </p>

      <h2 id="retention">How long we keep it</h2>
      <p>
        Membership and Mission Supporter records are kept for as long as the membership or listing
        is active, and afterward for as long as the Foundation's Document Retention and Destruction
        Policy requires. Records of a decision, including a decline or a revocation and its stated
        reason, are kept because the appeal process depends on them. An application that is never
        confirmed is not a membership, and we will remove it on request.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rights">Your choices</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask us what we hold about you, ask us to correct it, ask us to remove it, or ask us
        to take your organization off the Mission Supporter roster. Write to{' '}
        <a href="mailto:membership@openarcollective.org">membership@openarcollective.org</a> and a
        person will answer. We will not require you to justify the request.
      </p>
      <p>
        Removing your information generally ends the membership or the listing it supports, because
        the record is the membership. Where a record has to be kept for the Foundation's own
        governance, such as the record of a decision that is subject to appeal, we will tell you
        what is being kept and why rather than quietly keeping it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Depending on where you live, you may have rights under laws such as the General Data
        Protection Regulation or state privacy statutes in the United States. We will honor those
        requests through the same address regardless of where you are, because maintaining two
        standards would be more work than doing it properly once.
      </p>

      <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
      <p>
        Both sites are served over HTTPS. Access to the administrative system is limited to the
        people who run the membership program. We do not ask for and do not store payment details,
        government identifiers, or financial account information, because the Foundation charges
        nothing and has no reason to hold any of it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation's programs are for working professionals. The sites are not directed at
        children, and we do not knowingly collect information from anyone under sixteen.
      </p>

      <h2 id="changes">Changes to this notice</h2>
      <p>
        If we change what we collect or who processes it, we will update this notice. Material
        changes affecting current members will also go to the members-only email list.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this notice, or about anything the Foundation holds, go to{' '}
        <a href="mailto:membership@openarcollective.org">membership@openarcollective.org</a>. The
        Foundation is The Open Accounts Receivable Collective Foundation, a Delaware nonprofit
        corporation operating as The OpenAR Collective.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Long-form legal prose, so this matches the governance documents under
     /policies: same measure, same heading rhythm, same rule between sections.
     The global .prose class carries only a paragraph rule, which left every
     heading and the processor list with no spacing of their own at all. */
  .prose {
    max-width: 43rem;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 1.25rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-line);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }

  /* The first heading opens the document, so it needs no rule above it. */
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
    border-top: none;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--c-text-soft);
  }

  .prose ul {
    margin: 1rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--c-text-soft);
  }

  .prose li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
